15 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating control and hypoxia cognitive testing protocols over a fifteen-minute period with Stroop test in light gray, mental rotation task in medium gray, and post-exposure questionnaire in dark gray. Continuous subjective evaluation is marked with blue vertical lines, SpO2 monitoring with blue arrows, and a fifteen-minute break at minute eight.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 31 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 45 0 R /Alt (Two grouped bar charts labeled A and B compare participant counts for fifteen symptoms, numbered and listed below, across three intensity levels: one \(gray\), two \(light blue\), and three \(dark blue\). Symptom numbers correspond to specific symptoms such as anxiety, tingling in fingers, fatigue, and dizziness as shown in a key beneath the charts. Vertical axes indicate participant counts from zero to twelve, and symptom intensities are indicated in a legend at the top of each chart.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 46 0 R /Alt (Horizontal bar chart comparing the number of participants experiencing the emergence \(blue bars\) or disappearance \(gray bars\) of specific symptoms such as anxiety, headache, confusion, and shortness of breath, with most symptoms showing a greater number of participants reporting their emergence.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (Grouped bar chart panels labeled A to F display symptom intensity proportions over time in minutes, with each bar divided by intensity levels one to ten shown in varying shades of blue and gray. A color legend along the bottom labels each shade from one \(lightest\) to ten \(darkest\). All panels feature y-axes labeled \215Proportion of participants\216 and x-axes labeled \215Time \(min\),\216 showing temporal trends in symptom intensity distributions.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (Panel A shows a boxplot comparing accuracy percentage between control and hypoxia conditions, with individual data points connected by light gray lines and most values clustering near ninety five percent. Panel B presents a boxplot of reaction time in seconds across control and hypoxia conditions, similarly displaying individual data trajectories and a trend towards reduced reaction time in hypoxia. Panel C features a boxplot for corrected reaction time in seconds, comparing control and hypoxia, with individual lines indicating generally lower corrected times under hypoxia.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt (Three side-by-side box plots labeled A, B, and C compare control and hypoxia groups for three measures. Panel A shows accuracy percentages, Panel B displays reaction time in seconds, and Panel C presents corrected reaction time in seconds. Individual data points are connected by gray lines, and box plots include medians, quartiles, and whiskers, with outliers marked.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt <> /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Box plot comparing time in seconds between control and hypoxia groups, with paired lines for individual subjects. Control group times are generally higher; hypoxia group shows greater variability and lower median.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
